<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Porus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 317 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Porus was an Indian king who ruled the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a> rivers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. In 326 BCE he fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes">Battle of the Hydaspes</a> with a large army including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>, and was defeated. Impressed by his bearing, Alexander restored him to his kingdom and enlarged it. He was murdered by the Macedonian officer Eudemus around 317 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/porus.jpg" alt="Porus" width="320" height="323" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User PHGCOM · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porus_alexander_coin.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Porus is known only from Greek sources. His kingdom lay between the Hydaspes (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jhelum">Jhelum</a>) and Acesines (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chenab">Chenab</a>) rivers, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> noted that it held almost 300 cities. Scholars differ on who he was. Michael Witzel conjectures that he was a king of the Pūrus, a Vedic tribe, and Hem Chandra Raychaudhuri largely agreed. Ishwari Prasad and others argue that he was a Shurasena, because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-curtius-rufus">Quintus Curtius Rufus</a> says his vanguard carried a banner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Herakles</a>.</p><p>Porus was hostile to the neighbouring polity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taxila">Taxila</a>, whose earlier ruler Ambhiraj, his maternal uncle, he had assassinated. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Omphis</a>, the ruler of Taxila and son of Ambhiraj, greeted him and hoped to use his forces to bring Porus and Abisares to submission. Abisares offered submission, but Porus refused.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-hydaspes">Battle of the Hydaspes</a> in 326 BCE, Alexander crossed the river on a stormy night just before dawn, while the rest of his forces were spread along the bank to distract Porus. Porus took a defensive position on the plain, with infantry and elephants in front and cavalry and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariots</a> on the wings. Alexander's cavalry charged his left wing, and his cavalry was driven back into the infantry lines, causing confusion.</p><p>The mahouts were killed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarissa">sarissas</a> and the elephants pushed back into Porus' own columns. Porus refused to surrender and wandered on an elephant until he was wounded and his force routed. The exact site and strengths are unknown because the sources differ widely, and A. B. Bosworth warns that they are highly exaggerated. Alexander founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> at the site of the victory and Bucephalous in memory of his horse.</p><p>Alexander, impressed by Porus, did not depose him. He restored and enlarged his territory, annexing that of Glausaes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambhi">Omphis</a> was reconciled with him. Porus joined a campaign east of the Acesines against a cousin of his and took part in the siege of Sangala. When Alexander turned back at the Hyphasis, Porus was left as de facto ruler of the entire territory east of the Hydaspes, with no European satrap beside him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Alexander died in 323 BCE and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdiccas">Perdiccas</a> was murdered in 321 BCE, the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a>, according to Diodorus, recognised Porus' authority over the territories along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Eudemus, who had been Alexander's satrap in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, killed him. Porus reportedly died sometime between 321 and 315 BCE. In the medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-romance">Alexander Romance</a> he is defeated by Alexander in single combat, and he has appeared in many later films, series and games.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Porus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His conquest of the Achaemenid Persian Empire included victories at the Granicus (334 BCE), Issus (333 BCE) and Gaugamela (331 BCE), the sieges of Tyre and Gaza (332 BCE), and the defeat of <b>Porus</b> at the Hydaspes (326 BCE), successes in pitched battle and in siege warfare alike.</blockquote><a href="/empire/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its king, called <b>Porus</b> by the Greeks, perhaps from the Vedic clan name Puru, was a rival of Ambhi of the Kingdom of Taxila.</blockquote><a href="/state/paurava-kingdom">Paurava kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31,74&amp;year=-327" aria-label="Punjab on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6464 1034 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Porus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6914 1304h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6914 1304h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31,74&amp;year=-327">What was here in 327 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Treat me, O Alexander, in a kingly way!&#8221;</p><footer>Arrian, Anabasis, book 5 &#183; tr. E. J. Chinnock, 1884 &#183; asked by the victorious Alexander how he wished to be treated, 326 BCE; Arrian's account &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46976/pg46976-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 540 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
